  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">What the company actually does</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A company with sellable records usually hears from one buyer at a time, which makes it impossible to tell a fair offer from the only offer. DataLeasers changes the order of events.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Parallel conversations</strong>The same dataset goes to several buyers and AI labs at once, so what comes back can be compared on scope, term and price rather than accepted on faith.</li>
        <li><strong>Written terms</strong>What each buyer asks for is recorded, including the requests that are bad for a seller, such as long exclusivity or broad sublicensing.</li>
        <li><strong>Company approval</strong>Terms come back for the company to approve in writing. Nothing about the data moves before that signature.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="link-row reveal"><a href="how-it-works.html">How a deal runs</a> <a href="what-affects-a-deal.html">What changes the terms</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">How DataLeasers gets paid</h2>
      <p class="reveal">The data buyer pays DataLeasers when a company it introduced signs a licence. A company selling its data pays nothing for the introduction. That payment comes after signature, following processing of the deal.</p>
      <p class="reveal">The reason to state it plainly is that the incentive is visible: an introduction business earns only when a licence is signed, so it has no reason to talk a company out of a deal, and no reason to talk it into a bad one. Where that tension shows up, it is written on the page rather than left out.</p>
      <p class="link-row reveal"><a href="refer.html">Referral terms</a> <a href="editorial-standards.html">Editorial standards</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">What it will not do</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Sign on a company&rsquo;s behalf</strong>Every agreement is the company&rsquo;s own, reviewed by the company.</li>
        <li><strong>Take ownership of the data</strong>This is an introduction business. It does not buy corpora, resell them, or hold a sublicence over them.</li>
        <li><strong>Quote a price before seeing the corpus</strong>No honest figure exists before diligence, so none is offered.</li>
        <li><strong>Publish a figure it cannot trace</strong>Every number on this site carries a date and the page it came from.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="link-row reveal"><a href="rights-file.html">What you keep</a> <a href="buyer-terms.html">The buyer&rsquo;s terms</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

